---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<Base title="Surf">
  <div class="wrap">
    <section class="hero">
      <p class="brand-mark">Surf</p>
      <h1>A modern browser for devices that can't run one anymore</h1>
      <p class="deck">
        Surf runs Chromium on a computer and streams it to a native app on
        another device. The app works like a regular browser, with tabs, an
        address bar, bookmarks and history, while pages load and run in
        Chromium on the computer.
      </p>
      <ul class="cta-row">
        <li><a class="cta cta-primary" href={`${base}install/host/`}>Install the host</a></li>
        <li><a class="cta cta-secondary" href={`${base}install/ios/`}>iOS client</a></li>
        <li><a class="cta cta-secondary" href={`${base}install/desktop/`}>Linux client</a></li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>How it works</h2>
      <p>
        The host program on your computer keeps its own Chromium profile, so
        cookies, JavaScript, downloads and site sign-ins all live there. The
        app on your other device connects over TLS, shows the live stream, and
        sends your touch, keyboard and pointer input back, along with any files
        you upload. You pair the two once, with a QR code or a short code, and
        check that the same six words show up on both screens.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is no hosted service and no account. The host runs on your home
        network, or at any address your device can reach. Each device
        has its own key, and each client remembers the host's certificate and
        refuses to connect if it changes.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Using Surf</h2>
      <div class="point">
        <p class="point-title">Page compatibility</p>
        <p>
          Websites see an up-to-date desktop Chromium, so pages that no longer
          load on an old device work again. If a site has trouble with signing
          in through the stream, open Browser setup on the computer, sign in
          there, and continue from your device.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="point">
        <p class="point-title">Browser interface</p>
        <p>
          Tabs, suggestions, bookmarks, history, downloads, find and a reader
          view are built into the app. It shows the browser only, not the whole
          desktop.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="point">
        <p class="point-title">Data storage</p>
        <p>
          Pairings and the browser profile are stored in
          <code>SURF_HOME</code> on the computer. To remove a device, revoke
          it from the host.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Clients</h2>
      <div class="point">
        <p class="point-title">
          <a href={`${base}install/ios/`}>iOS / iPadOS 6-14</a>
        </p>
        <p>
          The iOS client is a jailbreak package for iOS 6 through 14 and
          installs from Cydia or Sileo. The steps are on the iOS page.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="point">
        <p class="point-title">
          <a href={`${base}install/desktop/`}>Linux</a>
        </p>
        <p>
          A desktop client that pairs with the host the same way the iOS app
          does.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="point">
        <p class="point-title">
          <a href="https://github.com/jmverges/surf-vita">PS Vita (community port)</a>
        </p>
        <p>
          A port of the shared client core, maintained in its own repository.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <h2>Docs</h2>
      <p>
        Start with the <a href={`${base}install/host/`}>host install</a>,
        <a href={`${base}docs/guide/pairing/`}>pairing</a>, the
        <a href={`${base}docs/guide/overview/`}>overview</a> and
        <a href={`${base}docs/reference/security/`}>security</a>. The
        <a href={`${base}docs/`}>full index</a> has the rest.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
